### A DEFECT THIS CASE PINS, not a behaviour it endorses.
###
### `reference` is declared `unique`. Sending a value another row already holds is an ordinary caller
### mistake — the same class as a missing required field or a bad enum value — and every one of those
### is answered with a 422 naming the field. A duplicate is answered **500**, because `unique` is the
### one declared facet the framework does not validate: it is enforced only by the database, and the
### provider's unique-violation exception is not mapped onto `IAlvoData`'s refusal families.
###
### The case asserts what happens TODAY, deliberately and labelled, for two reasons. It goes red the
### day the defect is fixed, which forces this file to be updated rather than letting the fix ship
### unnoticed. And it pins the one thing that must stay true meanwhile: the 500 discloses nothing —
### no exception type, no SQL, no constraint name, no stack frame.
###
### Two consequences, both worth stating where the next reader will meet them:
###   1. An agent cannot repair the request. There is no violation, no pointer and no fix suggestion,
###      and the detail says only "an internal error"; the field at fault is not named at all.
###   2. On a tenant-SCOPED entity a globally-unique field is a cross-tenant existence oracle. Tenant
###      B can learn whether tenant A holds a given `reference` by watching 500 versus 201 — which is
###      exactly the inference the 404-everywhere design elsewhere is careful to prevent. 080-Tenancy
###      measures the same probe from tenant south.
# @name DuplicateUniqueValue
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [500]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-1001",
  "title": "Duplicate reference",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "access_code": "DUP",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The comparison that makes the point: change nothing but the unique value and the create succeeds.
### So the 500 above is the duplicate and nothing else about the payload.
# @name SameBodyFreshReference
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-3900",
  "title": "Duplicate reference",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 3,
  "access_code": "DUP",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### And the failed create left nothing behind
# @name AfterTheDuplicate
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?reference=eq.WO-1001&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The comparison row is removed again, so every later group measures the seeded seven and not an
### eighth row this case happened to leave behind.
# @name RemoveTheComparisonRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{SameBodyFreshReference.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

###
# @name BackToTheSeededSet
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
